@mguan2020 I believe want to change the behaviour of `inspect` by default, not introduce a new method.

@p8 I think your solution will not solve the performance issue we are experiencing, since it still requires us to hit the parameter filter for every attribute.

I opened a PR with a solution that allows the attributes used by the `inspect` method to be configured: https://github.com/rails/rails/pull/49765

review_comments:
@paracycle The call site is often in gem / stdlib code, not application code. So I believe the developer would be locked into just inspecting with in `id` in those cases. The original issue came up in a case where we call `to_s` on a hash containing AR records, which calls inspect on the records. Also, it doesn't help make the behaviour easily opt out-able, since the developer would have to find all the call sites and add the `:all` parameter - and in cases like the above it wouldn't be possible to opt out.

Tho maybe it would be nice to have the ability to pick different attributes at specific call sites, and use the model's `attributes_to_inspect` as the default. It's not exactly the problem I am trying to solve, but I could see it being useful.

The name `attributes_to_list` is good thank you, I am going to use that.

@adrianna-chang-shopify Thanks for clarifying the `:all` option vs array distinction. After chewing it over I like the idea so I will hack on it sometime today or tomorrow 👍
